answer.javascript:
**Approach: backtracking over compatible sets.**

1. `pool = playableWords(words)` sorted by text: no double letters, no duplicate spellings.
2. Depth-first search. Each call is one board: record it if it beats the best (higher total, or equal total with a smaller word list), then try every later word that fits.

```javascript
function search(start, used, total) {
  if (total > bestTotal || (total === bestTotal && listBefore(chosen, bestWords))) {
    bestTotal = total;
    bestWords = chosen.slice();
  }
  for (let i = start; i < pool.length; i++) {
    if (pool[i].mask & used) continue;
    chosen.push(pool[i].text);
    search(i + 1, used | pool[i].mask, total + pool[i].length);
    chosen.pop();
  }
}
```

Masks make the compatibility check one `&`, and because words are added in alphabetical order, `chosen` is always sorted, so the tie-break is a word-by-word comparison (`listBefore`).

**Complexity.** O(B · n), where B is the number of compatible word sets (at most 2^n) and n the number of candidates. With about 20 candidates that's at most a few million steps. Greedy by length fails on `blanket / trophy / jumbled / fix`.

