answer.javascript:
**Where phase 2 breaks.** It visits every compatible set of words. Short-word packs have millions of them, because boards are eight or more words deep. In long-word packs every letter set has up to eight spellings, so each real board is enumerated as up to 8^k spelling variants. Either way it runs for seconds.

**Optimized approach.**

1. **One entry per letter set.** Drop double-letter words and keep, for each mask, the alphabetically first spelling. Swapping a spelling for a smaller one with the same letters always gives a smaller sorted list, so the answer can't change.
2. **Branch and bound for the best total**, longest words first. Each call receives only the candidates that still fit, and `reach[p]` (the union of `candidates[p..]`) bounds what the board can still gain:

```javascript
function grow(candidates, total) {
  if (total > best) best = total;
  const reach = reachOf(masks, candidates); // suffix unions
  for (let p = 0; p < candidates.length; p++) {
    if (total + popcount(reach[p]) <= best) return;
    const i = candidates[p];
    grow(compatible(masks, candidates, p, masks[i]), total + sizes[i]);
  }
}
```

3. **Tie-break pass.** Search again over the entries in alphabetical order, pruning when `total + popcount(reach[p]) < target`, and return the first board that reaches `target`. Depth-first search over alphabetically sorted words visits boards in word-list order, and no two boards with the same total can be prefixes of each other. So the first board that reaches the target is the tie-break winner.

**Result.** Both large packs drop from several seconds to a few milliseconds.

**Complexity and tradeoffs.** The worst case is still exponential, as it must be for set packing, but the 26-letter universe makes the bound tight: once the best board is within a few letters of every letter that is still reachable, whole subtrees vanish. A DP over reachable letter unions is an alternative. It is fast on long-word packs (tens of thousands of unions), but the large short-word pack reaches millions of distinct unions and takes seconds.

